{"subjectId":"c","id":"operators","title":"Operators & Precedence","group":"Types, Variables & Operators","order":9,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C’s operators are compact and powerful. Most bugs come from precedence surprises, integer division, and unsequenced side effects — use parentheses generously.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d %d %d %d\\n\", 17 / 5, 17 % 5, -17 / 5, -17 % 5);   /* truncates toward 0 */\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", 17 / 5.0);                                   /* one double → real division */\n    int i = 5;\n    int a = i++;      /* a = 5, then i = 6 */\n    int b = ++i;      /* i = 7, then b = 7 */\n    printf(\"a=%d b=%d i=%d\\n\", a, b, i);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 2 -3 -2\n3.40\na=5 b=7 i=7</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Integer <code>/</code> truncates toward zero (since C99) and <code>%</code> takes the sign of the left operand, so <code>(a / b) * b + a % b == a</code> always holds.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-compare\"></i>Relational, logical and short-circuit</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint calls = 0;\nint check(int v) { calls++; return v; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", 3 &gt; 2, 3 == 2, !5);      /* results are int 1 or 0 */\n    if (check(0) &amp;&amp; check(1)) { }                 /* right side skipped */\n    if (check(1) || check(1)) { }                 /* right side skipped */\n    printf(\"calls = %d\\n\", calls);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 0 0\ncalls = 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Assignment, comma, ternary, sizeof</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 10;\n    x += 5; x *= 2; x -= 6; x /= 4; x %= 4;\n    printf(\"x = %d\\n\", x);\n\n    int a, b;\n    a = b = 7;                          /* assignment is an expression, right-assoc */\n    int max = (a &gt; 3) ? a : 3;          /* conditional operator */\n    int last = (a++, b++, a + b);       /* comma: evaluate left, yield right */\n    printf(\"%d %d %d %d\\n\", a, b, max, last);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>x = 2\n8 8 7 16</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Precedence table (high → low)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Operators</th><th>Associativity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>()</code> <code>[]</code> <code>-&gt;</code> <code>.</code> postfix <code>++</code> <code>--</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>prefix <code>++</code> <code>--</code>, unary <code>+</code> <code>-</code> <code>!</code> <code>~</code>, cast <code>(type)</code>, <code>*</code> (deref), <code>&amp;</code> (address), <code>sizeof</code></td><td>right → left</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>*</code> <code>/</code> <code>%</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>+</code> <code>-</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>&lt;&lt;</code> <code>&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>==</code> <code>!=</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><code>^</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>|</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><code>&amp;&amp;</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><code>||</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><code>?:</code></td><td>right → left</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><code>=</code> <code>+=</code> <code>-=</code> <code>*=</code> <code>/=</code> <code>%=</code> <code>&lt;&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;&gt;=</code> <code>&amp;=</code> <code>^=</code> <code>|=</code></td><td>right → left</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td><code>,</code></td><td>left → right</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Classic precedence traps</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — compiled with -Wno-parentheses to let the traps through</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int flags = 6;\n    /* == binds tighter than &amp;:  flags &amp; (4 == 4)  →  6 &amp; 1  →  0 */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", flags &amp; 4 == 4 ? 1 : 0);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", (flags &amp; 4) == 4 ? 1 : 0);\n\n    /* + binds tighter than &lt;&lt; :  1 &lt;&lt; (2 + 1) */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", 1 &lt;&lt; 2 + 1);\n\n    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};\n    int *p = arr;\n    int v = *p++;      /* *(p++): read 10, then advance p */\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", v, *p);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0\n1\n8\n10 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>i = i++ + ++i;</code> or <code>printf(\"%d %d\", i, i++)</code> modify and read the same variable without a <em>sequence point</em> — that is <strong>undefined behaviour</strong>, not “compiler-dependent output”. Clang rejects it under <code>-Werror</code> (<code>-Wunsequenced</code>).</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — unsequenced modification (rejected)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = 1;\n    i = i++ + ++i;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", i);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between i++ and ++i?</summary><div><p>Both add 1 to <code>i</code>. The <em>value of the expression</em> differs: <code>i++</code> yields the old value, <code>++i</code> yields the new value. As a standalone statement (<code>i++;</code>) they are identical and compile to the same code.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does short-circuit evaluation mean?</summary><div><p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> stops as soon as the left side is false, <code>||</code> stops as soon as the left side is true, and there is a sequence point after the left operand. That makes guards like <code>if (p != NULL &amp;&amp; p-&gt;count &gt; 0)</code> safe.</p></div></details></section>"}